agues. So strengths: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence (weather, computers). Concerns: interpersonal (nasty remarks) and memory for numbers (attention/accuracy). Could affect crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. Weaknesses: difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education. So strengths: decision quality, stress resilience, technical competence. Concerns: communication, teamwork, adaptability.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Weaknesses: regarded as a know-it-all, has a hot temper. So strengths: reliability, stress resilience, problem solving, responsibility. Concerns: temperament, cooperation.\n\nWe need to pick best overall fit. All have some concerns. Which candidate has strongest overall? Let's weigh.\n\nCandidate B: strong reliability, stress, technical, but interpersonal issues (nasty remarks) could be serious for crew cooperation. That may be a red flag.\n\nCandidate C: strong decision, calm, technical, but communication issues and egocentric, not willing to learn. That also problematic.\n\nCandidate D: strong problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but hot temper and know-it-all attitude could affect crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate A: strong anticipation, spatial vision, leadership, but show-off and not open to new ideas. Also maybe less evidence on stress resilience.\n\nWhich is best? Possibly Candidate D has good overall reliability and responsibility, but hot temper is concerning for crew cooperation. Candidate B's nasty remarks also severe. Candidate C's communication issues also severe. Candidate A's show-off may be less severe than outright nasty remarks. But not open to new ideas is a concern for adaptability.\n\nWe need to argue for a candidate. I think Candidate D might be the best despite hot temper, because strengths cover many criteria: responsibility, concentration, problem solving, handling unexpected events, which are crucial for long-distance flights. The hot temper could be mitigated with training. Others have more severe interpersonal issues (nasty remarks, egocentric, communication difficulty). So I will recommend Candidate D.\n\nProvide evidence: D responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience), concentrates well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical competence), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility). Concern: hot temper and know-it-all (cooperation). But overall fit stronger.\n\nThus my vote: Candidate D.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE: state recommendation, reference evidence, note concerns, compare with others briefly.\n\nMETADATA_JSON with vote."}
